WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.753 --> 00:00:22.915
Hi, Little Fox readers! We've been learning about the environment, so Mr. Fay told us about a landmark that goes perfectly with this topic—Easter Island.

2
00:00:22.916 --> 00:00:30.296
Also known as Rapa Nui, this island in the South Pacific is the most remote inhabited island in the world.

3
00:00:30.297 --> 00:00:37.519
And it's famous for something else: hundreds of huge, mysterious statues called moai.

4
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:44.970
At first I couldn't imagine how a tiny island with a bunch of statues is connected to taking care of the environment.

5
00:00:44.971 --> 00:00:50.310
But I found out that these statues have a lot to do with the environment.

6
00:00:50.311 --> 00:00:58.547
I'll get to that in a minute. First let me tell you about the island itself—it has a really interesting history!

7
00:00:58.548 --> 00:01:04.396
Easter Island was first settled by Polynesians sometime around 400 AD.

8
00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:15.733
The island's landscape is very sparse now, but when those settlers, now called the Rapanui, first arrived in their canoes, the whole island was covered in palm trees.

9
00:01:15.734 --> 00:01:26.619
Nobody is sure why these people came to the island. I'm amazed that they even found it because it's small—only 163 square kilometers.

10
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:33.444
And it's in the middle of nowhere, almost 2,000 kilometers from Pitcairn Island, the nearest land.

11
00:01:33.445 --> 00:01:40.872
Over time the Rapanui changed the island's landscape by cutting down trees and clearing the land to plant crops.

12
00:01:40.873 --> 00:01:54.242
And between 1000 and 1680 AD, they transformed the landscape in another dramatic way—by sculpting hundreds of moai and placing them all over the island.

13
00:01:54.243 --> 00:01:59.241
The Rapanui carved the moai out of yellowish-gray volcanic rock.

14
00:01:59.242 --> 00:02:08.010
The heads look a little creepy but cool too, with long noses and ears, and protruding foreheads above deep-set eyes.

15
00:02:08.011 --> 00:02:13.922
Archaeologists believe these statues depict important Rapanui ancestors and chiefs.

16
00:02:13.923 --> 00:02:18.809
Some of the moai also have round topknots on top of their heads.

17
00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:24.696
The topknots look like big red hats because they're made from red volcanic rock.

18
00:02:24.697 --> 00:02:30.828
Most of the moai were carved out of the bedrock of the volcano Rano Raraku.

19
00:02:30.829 --> 00:02:39.639
First, sculptors shaped the statues' faces and heads. When that was done, the statues were cut free from the surrounding rock.

20
00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:48.037
Next, they were lowered down the slope of the volcano and propped up vertically in shallow holes so the backs could be carved.

21
00:02:48.038 --> 00:02:53.894
Today there are 397 moai remaining at Rano Raraku.

22
00:02:53.895 --> 00:03:00.137
Some are still attached to the bedrock, including one that's over 20 meters long!

23
00:03:00.138 --> 00:03:10.943
Others are standing up in those shallow holes. But because of silt running down the slope of the volcano over so many years, they're partially buried.

24
00:03:10.944 --> 00:03:15.789
Completed moai had to be moved to various villages around the island.

25
00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:24.631
This must have been extremely difficult since the average moai was over 4 meters tall and weighed over 12 metric tons.

26
00:03:24.632 --> 00:03:30.338
Moving one probably required between 50 and 150 people!

27
00:03:30.339 --> 00:03:34.850
Nobody knows for sure exactly how the moai were moved.

28
00:03:34.851 --> 00:03:45.918
Many people believe they were placed on wooden sleds that were towed with ropes, while others believe that the statues were tilted slightly and then "walked" across the island.

29
00:03:45.919 --> 00:03:56.245
My favorite theory is one that Mr. Fay told us, from an island legend: The moai got up and walked by themselves!

30
00:03:56.246 --> 00:04:01.139
Once moved, moai were placed on rectangular stone platforms.

31
00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:05.874
Most of these platforms were placed near the coast, overlooking villages.

32
00:04:05.875 --> 00:04:12.490
Each one had between one and fifteen moai standing in a row, with their backs to the sea.

33
00:04:12.491 --> 00:04:17.220
These moai were given eyes made from shells and polished stones.

34
00:04:17.221 --> 00:04:31.051
The Rapanui had no contact with other people until Easter Sunday, 1722, when a Dutch explorer named Jacob Roggeveen arrived on the island and started calling it Easter Island.

35
00:04:31.052 --> 00:04:35.198
Roggeveen wrote that the islanders worshipped the moai.

36
00:04:35.199 --> 00:04:44.701
He also wrote that, unlike most Polynesian islands, Easter Island had almost no trees, just small shrubs and grass.

37
00:04:44.702 --> 00:04:54.370
Sometime after Roggeveen's arrival, a civil war broke out on the island, probably because of overpopulation and limited resources.

38
00:04:54.371 --> 00:05:00.377
The Rapanui stopped making moai and even started knocking each others' down!

39
00:05:00.378 --> 00:05:08.774
Today Easter Island is part of Rapa Nui National Park. Visitors can get there from Santiago, Chile.

40
00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:17.363
Almost everyone on the island lives in Hanga Roa, the island's only town, which has some hotels, restaurants, and shops.

41
00:05:17.364 --> 00:05:27.442
Mr. Fay said that there are about nine hundred moai still scattered all over the island, so it's a good idea to hire a guide who can show you around.

42
00:05:27.443 --> 00:05:35.888
One very popular site for tourists is the quarry at Rano Raraku, where you can see the unfinished moai.

43
00:05:35.889 --> 00:05:40.245
I bet you're still wondering what Easter Island has to do with the environment.

44
00:05:40.246 --> 00:05:49.230
Well, many experts believe the Rapanui continued cutting down trees to use as firewood and building materials for many years.

45
00:05:49.231 --> 00:05:53.076
They also used them as tools for moving the giant moai.

46
00:05:53.077 --> 00:05:58.985
Eventually every single tree on the entire island was gone!

47
00:05:58.986 --> 00:06:05.548
Without trees the soil suffered from severe erosion and became depleted of its nutrients.

48
00:06:05.549 --> 00:06:12.444
Farming the land became difficult. I asked Mr. Fay why the Rapanui didn't leave.

49
00:06:12.445 --> 00:06:24.196
He said they probably couldn't. To leave, they would have needed canoes. And what do you need to make canoes? Yep, you got it—trees!

50
00:06:24.197 --> 00:06:33.102
The story of Easter Island shows us just how important it is to take care of the environment and use natural resources wisely.

51
00:06:33.103 --> 00:06:38.179
I was really glad to learn that new trees are now being planted on Easter Island.

52
00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:45.495
Maybe someday palm trees will cover the island again, just as they did when the Rapanui first arrived.

53
00:06:45.496 --> 00:06:53.439
After our landmarks lesson, we got to make our own moai out of clay. I made mine standing on a platform.

54
00:06:53.440 --> 00:07:01.234
It looked a little sad all by itself, so I added something else: a tall, leafy palm tree!

55
00:07:01.235 --> 00:07:03.762
See you next week!
